# JWT authentication module

Installed by `pfw add auth` or `pfw new -template api -auth` for stdlib and Echo.
The application owns the generated types and can extend Principal or replace
any component through the generic security contracts.

```text
Authorization: Bearer JWT → BearerCredentials → VerifiedJWT → Principal
```

Authentication uses no database, repository or migration. The existing API item
storage is independent of this module. The flow is identical in all environments.

- `GET /auth/me`: requires a valid JWT and returns the principal.
- `GET /auth/status`: accepts anonymous requests, but rejects invalid credentials.
- Existing application routes keep their access rules. Apply the injected `securityhttp.HandlerMiddleware.Required`
  (stdlib) or `pfwecho.RequiredSecurity` (Echo) to your chosen routes and enforce
  resource policies in application services.

## Application-owned JWT example

This scaffold is a starting point to adapt to your application. The framework
provides security contracts and HTTP middleware; these generated files own the
HS256 implementation, claims, principal and configuration. No login flow,
user storage or signing secret is generated.

- `principal.go`: credentials, JWT claims, verified identity and application principal.
- `bearer.go`: Authorization header extraction and HTTP Bearer challenge.
- `jwt.go`: JWT verification and startup configuration validation.
- `resolver.go`: verified identity to application principal mapping.
- `config/config.go`: JWT settings and startup validation.
- `infrastructure/http/controller.go`: example auth endpoints.

## Configuration and local example

The API reads the project's `env/` files. No terminal
exports are needed. Issuer and audience default to `palma-api` and are documented
in `.env.example` and the environment-specific files.

Choose a random signing secret of at least 32 bytes. To obtain one locally:

```sh
openssl rand -base64 32
```

Paste the result into `env/.env.local.local` (ignored by Git):

```dotenv
APP_AUTH_JWT_SECRET=<paste your random secret here>
```

The loader reads `env/.env`, `env/.env.local` and `env/.env.local.local`, in that
order; process variables can override them. For staging and production, supply
separate secrets in `.env.staging.local` and `.env.prod.local` or through your
deployment's configuration. Never commit signing secrets.

From the project root:

```sh
go mod tidy
go tool pfw generate -env local
go test ./...
go tool pfw run -env local
```

To call `/auth/me`, obtain a JWT from your application's authentication flow
that matches the configured signing key, issuer, audience and claims. Token
issuance belongs to the application.

```sh
curl -H 'Authorization: Bearer <your JWT>' http://localhost:8080/auth/me
curl http://localhost:8080/auth/status
curl -i -H 'Authorization: Bearer invalid' http://localhost:8080/auth/status
```

A missing or shorter-than-32-byte secret stops API startup in every environment
with a configuration error. There is no demo signing secret or fallback identity.

## Verification and principal

The authenticator uses `github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5` with an explicit HS256
allowlist. It verifies signature, required expiration, issuer and audience,
rejects an empty subject, and validates `nbf`/`iat` when present. Malformed,
expired, unsigned or wrongly signed tokens produce 401 with a Bearer challenge.
The [library documentation](https://golang-jwt.github.io/jwt/usage/parse/)
explains these parser options.

Only after verification does the resolver map `sub` to `Principal.UserID` and
copy `roles` into `Principal.Roles`. Roles must be an array of strings. No user
lookup or role query is performed. Extend the typed Claims, VerifiedJWT and
Principal types to carry application-specific data.

`internal/bootstrap/auth_module.go` registers the framework's instantiated
`securityhttp.NewMiddleware` constructor directly. The bootstrap discovers
annotated application constructors and `AutoBind` resolves their security
contracts. Controllers receive `HandlerMiddleware` (stdlib) or
`RequestAuthenticator` (Echo), so a custom implementation can be selected with
`pfw.Bind` or a preferred constructor with `pfw.Override`. The shared mapper handles
401/403 without exposing internal causes. Repeating `pfw add auth` preserves
customizations; it does not upgrade a previously installed auth recipe.

HS256 is the example's chosen method. Integrating an external issuer with
asymmetric keys/JWKS requires replacing or extending the authenticator and its
configuration. Login, refresh tokens and revocation are application features.
